{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "3 - Match State and Round Rules",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "A tiny vocabulary for the game",
      "description": "A match is the whole game between participants. A round is one coding problem within that match. A phase describes what is happening now, for example coding or reveal. A snapshot is a saved view of the relevant state at one moment."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/arena-game/src/records.ts",
      "line": 45,
      "title": "1. One round's state",
      "description": "RoundState keeps the data for a round: submissions counted for each side, attempt counts, activity, and the reveal once it is published. Optional readiness and shared-document fields support 2v2. Read the comments as a guide to why those fields exist."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/arena-game/src/records.ts",
      "line": 70,
      "title": "2. The whole match",
      "description": "MatchRecord wraps the rounds and adds match-wide information such as mode, current round, participants, revision, presence, and forfeit state. Follow the field types when you want to learn what data the game considers durable."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/arena-game/src/round-lifecycle.ts",
      "line": 57,
      "title": "3. Moving from one phase to another",
      "description": "RoundLifecycle groups the rules for starting a round, entering coding, closing submissions, revealing results, and advancing. For example, beginReveal closes the round to new submissions while already-running evaluations can settle during the grace window."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/arena-game/src/round-lifecycle.ts",
      "line": 120,
      "title": "4. The reveal is a snapshot",
      "description": "createReveal builds an immutable result containing this round's scores, group results, totals, and publish time. Keeping a snapshot makes the published result stable even as the next round begins."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/arena-game/src/round-lifecycle.ts",
      "line": 186,
      "title": "5. How a verdict becomes a score",
      "description": "scoreVerdict converts test outcomes and group weights into a score. Infrastructure problems are not treated as ordinary competitive failures. The exact arithmetic is kept in the lifecycle/domain code rather than in the screen."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/arena-game/src/engine.ts",
      "line": 1115,
      "title": "6. A Match deadline saves Reveal, then completes the Match",
      "description": "After expiry, the server closes the current Round; accepted work can settle only inside the bounded reveal grace. It saves the Reveal and terminal MATCH_COMPLETE state, emits events after the save, and computes the final score from saved Round records. Expiry in MATCH_FOUND or ROUND_INTRO still creates a zero-score Reveal; later Rounds remain at zero."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/arena-game/test/deadline-closure.test.ts",
      "line": 10,
      "title": "7. Check deadline closure with a fixed clock",
      "description": "These cases cover exact-deadline rejection, a zero-score Reveal before coding, a pre-deadline Submit that settles within grace, and a late result that is excluded. A terminal Match needs no player action to commit."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/arena-game/test/round-lifecycle.test.ts",
      "line": 43,
      "title": "8. Check Round rules with examples",
      "description": "This file tests lifecycle and scoring rules with small MatchRecord examples. Try reading one test as a sentence: given this initial phase and these outcomes, when this method runs, what should change?"
    },
    {
      "title": "Checkpoint — draw the Round from memory",
      "description": "Write down the phase sequence you remember from coding to the next round. Then explain which data belongs to one RoundState and which belongs to the whole MatchRecord. Tour 4 looks inside judging and sandbox boundaries."
    }
  ]
}
